{"reference":"Jeremiah 8","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":8,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Wider Portrait of Catastrophe","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The wider portrayal in Jeremiah has been read as using images of wounds, tears, siege, displacement, unburied bodies, and fractured memory to depict both catastrophe and the struggle to survive it. This disputed reading can help frame the chapter’s closing cries of grief and failed healing as part of a larger pattern of language that gives suffering concrete, bodily form.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_008_88f0f375a081a3aceba127a2"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}